# Development of DopantâFree Organic Hole Transporting Materials for Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
There has been considerable progress over the last decade in development of the perovskite solar cells (PSCs), with reported performances now surpassing 25.2% power conversion efficiency. Both long‐term stability and component costs of PSCs remain to be addressed by the research community, using hole transporting materials (HTMs) such as 2,2′,7,7′‐tetrakis(N,N′‐di‐pmethoxyphenylamino)‐9,9′‐spirbiuorene(Spiro‐OMeTAD) and poly[bis(4‐phenyl)(2,4,6‐trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA). HTMs are essential for high‐performance PSC devices. Although effective, these materials require a relatively high degree of doping with additives to improve charge mobility and interlayer/substrate compatibility, introducing doping‐induced stability issues with these HTMs, and further, additional costs and experimental complexity associated with using these doped materials. This article reviews dopant‐free organic HTMs for PSCs, outlining reports of structures with promising properties toward achieving low‐cost, effective, and scalable materials for devices with long‐term stability. It summarizes recent literature reports on non‐doped, alternative, and more stable HTMs used in PSCs as essential components for high‐efficiency cells, categorizing HTMs as reported for different PSC architectures in addition to use of dopant‐free small molecular and polymeric HTMs. Finally, an outlook and critical assessment of dopant‐free organic HTMs toward commercial application and insight into the development of stable PSC devices is provided.

## Development of Dopant-Free Organic Hole Transporting Materials for Perovskite Solar Cells
There has been considerable progress over the last decade in development of the perovskite solar cells (PSCs), with reported performances now surpassing $25.2\%$ power conversion efficiency. Both long-term stability and component costs of PSCs remain to be addressed by the research community, using hole transporting materials (HTMs) such as $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis $(N,N^{\prime}$ -di-pmethoxyphenylamino)- ${\mathfrak{s}},{\mathfrak{s}}^{\prime}$ -spirbiuorene(Spiro-OMeTAD) and poly[bis(4-phenyl) (2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA). HTMs are essential for highperformance PSC devices. Although effective, these materials require a relatively high degree of doping with additives to improve charge mobility and interlayer/substrate compatibility, introducing doping-induced stability issues with these HTMs, and further, additional costs and experimental complexity associated with using these doped materials. This article reviews dopant-free organic HTMs for PSCs, outlining reports of structures with promising properties toward achieving low-cost, effective, and scalable materials for devices with long-term stability. It summarizes recent literature reports on non-doped, alternative, and more stable HTMs used in PSCs as essential components for high-efficiency cells, categorizing HTMs as reported for different PSC architectures in addition to use of dopant-free small molecular and polymeric HTMs. Finally, an outlook and critical assessment of dopant-free organic HTMs toward commercial application and insight into the development of stable PSC devices is provided.
## 1. Introduction
In today’s world, the depletion of fossil fuels to meet the demands of global energy as well as environmental sustainability presents severe threats to the survival of humanity. For instance, forecasts for the growth of population and economic development predict, the energy demand of the world by 2040 will be over 17  700 million tonnes of oil equivalent (Mtoe), a $26\%$ increase compared to 13 972 Mtoe in 2017. Electricity demand increases by $2.1\%$ per year, which is double the rate of overall energy demand.[1] In order to reduce the reliance on fossil fuel energy, there are various types of renewable energy resources that have been invented and studied. Apart from conventional energy resources, nonconventional energy or renewable energy resources such as solar, wind, tidal, and biomass energy, have been considered as suitable alternatives to solve the energy crisis. In particular, intense research effort on various solar cell technologies is currently being carried out with the main aim of both reducing the cost and increasing the power conversion efficiency (PCE) for improving large-scale commercial manufacturing.
Solar cell technology is generally categorized into three main generations as illustrated in Figure 1. The first generation includes single-junction crystalline silicon solar cells which are relatively expensive with a PCE that is limited by the Shockley–Queisser law. The second generation includes copper indium gallium selenide, cadmium telluride, and amorphous silicon solar cells, which are less expensive but have lower efficiency than first generation technology. The third generation uses low-cost solar cells such as organic, dye-sensitized, and perovskite solar cells (PSCs), which offer several advantages, such as low cost, easy fabrication, flexibility, and the possibility to use scalable processing techniques, for instance, inkjet printing. The third generation also covers advanced technologies that exceed the Shockley– Queisser limit of $31\%$ efficiency with proven concepts such as multijunction or tandem structures through to more theoretical concepts such as hot carrier, down-/upconversion, and multiple carrier generation effects.[2] Compared to organic and dye-sensitized solar cells, PSCs have recently attracted
significant attention in the scientific community due to their ability to reach performances close to that of existing high-performance silicon- and other inorganic material-based solar cell technologies. In particular, the power conversion efficiency of PSC devices has already surpassed $25\%$ a milestone achieved in only 10 years of development.[3,4] Moreover, considering the cost/efficiency ratio, PSC technology is very attractive for global future energy needs.
Organic–inorganic halide perovskite (OIHP) materials have received significant attention in the optoelectronics community and they have become one of the most popular active materials chosen for this research topic. The chemical structure can be represented as $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ (as illustrated in Figure  2)[5] where A is a monovalent cation species such as $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{CH}^{+}$ , or ${\mathrm{Cs^{+}}}$ , B is a divalent cation species such as $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ , and X is a monovalent anion such as $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ , or a combination thereof. Owing to long charge carrier diffusion lengths, highly efficient ambipolar charge transport, wide optical absorption, and large absorption coefficients, $[5-8]$ OIHP materials have been intensively utilized in thin-film transistors,[9] lasers,[10] ambipolar transistor,[11] light-emitting diodes,[12] flexible memory,[13] and especially in solar cells, the topic of this review.[5,14] In addition to these applications, PSC research and commercial interest have intensified recently due to their ability to reach PCEs close to that of existing high-performance silicon and other inorganic material-based solar cell technologies.
Seminal reports on PSCs began with the initial efforts of Miyasaka and colleagues, who attempted to use perovskite structures, including methylammonium lead iodide $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ or $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}\mathsf{)}$ and methylammonium lead bromide $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b B r_{3}}$ or $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ ) as light-harvesting layers (sensitizers) of dyesynthesized solar cells in 2009.[15] They achieved a PCE of $3.8\%$ with an iodide/triiodide-based redox shuttle and liquid electrolyte. In 2011, Park and co-workers enhanced the PCE of the liquid-based $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cell to $6.5\%$ under standard AM 1.5G irradiation by modifying the titania surface and the processing method for deposition of the perovskite absorber layer.[16] However, these devices were not stable and degraded quickly because perovskite compounds dissolved in liquid electrolytes.[17] Subsequently, a significant breakthrough was achieved by the use of solid-state PSCs developed independently by the groups of both Snaith and Grätzel in 2012. The authors used $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Cl}_{x}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ as mixed-halide compositions of the photoactive material deposited onto a mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, in conjunction with the hole transport layer (HTL) $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis( $\mathbf{\widetilde{\Gamma}}N,N^{\prime}$ - di-pmethoxyphenylamino)- ${\cdot9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirbiuorene  (Spiro-OMeTAD), resulting in PCEs of $7.6\%$ and $9.7\%$ , respectively.[18] In a similar direction in the same year (2012), Snaith and co-workers demonstrated the fabrication of efficient PSCs by replacing the semiconducting $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by the insulator ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ .[19] Since, then, in less than seven years, the highest conversion efficiencies of PSCs has surged to $25.2\%$ in 2019.[3] The improvement of PSC efficiency over time is summarized in Figure 3.
