In this review, we first summarize factors affecting perovskite device degradation. Subsequently, we present a discussion on the role of dopant-free organic hole transporting materials (HTMs) employed in different architectures. Finally, we outline future perspectives from a materials chemistry viewpoint, outlining the correlation between chemical structure and inherent device stability.
## 2. Environmental Factors Impacting Stability of Devices
So far there have been several reports highlighting conditions that lead to instability of PSCs. In this section, we discuss the effects of moisture, temperature, and UV exposure on perovskite stability. Indeed, these main factors primarily give rise to decomposition of the perovskite material itself under typical working conditions in ambient air.
First of all, moisture is considered as a key issue causing decomposing of the perovskite during its fabrication in ambient air.[33] The proposed decomposition pathway of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ perovskite in the presence of moisture, as first reported by Frost et  al., is depicted in Figure  5a.[34] Fundamentally, it consists of the following steps: (a) the formation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ occurs when moisture $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ reaches the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ layer of the devices, (b) due to the unstable state, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ then decomposes into $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and HI. At this step, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and UV light can easily react with HI (c,d), which then causes the complete degradation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ perovskite.
In the case of thermal stability, the decomposition of the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite can be described as follows: $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{2}}+\mathrm{PbI_{2}}+\mathrm{HI.^{[35,36}}$ ] Additionally, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ formation has also been observed when the more stable triple/mixed-cation perovskite materials were degraded.[37] The proposed degradation of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ is illustrated in Figure 5a. The additional point is that the interface between the perovskite and electron transporting layer suffers from thermal instability. For instance, the PSC devices incorporating $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ as the ETM exhibited very low efficiency due to the unstable use of $\mathsf{Z n O}$ .[38]
Furthermore, perovskite materials can be decomposed by exposure to UV illumination as shown in Figure  5b,c.[39] Additionally, it has been reported that the perovskite converts to hexagonal $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ during the light exposure test.[39,40] The presence of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the perovskite film has been the subject of debate as to whether its impact in PSCs is beneficial or detrimental,[41] including in some cases where the impact of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ can be reversible.[42] Returning to stability, $\mathrm{Pb}$ exists in its $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ oxidation state in lead-based PSCs and because $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is soluble in water, it can severely hamper the stability of these devices by rapid degradation of the perovskite absorber when solar cells are kept in a humid atmosphere.[43] Furthermore, the degradation of device performance after prolonged illumination was caused by the photocatalysis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .[44]
## 3. Organic Hole Transporting Materials
The purpose of HTMs in PSCs is to extract and transport holes at the perovskite/HTM interface after they have been generated in the perovskite absorber layer. The energy alignment between the HTM and perovskite is crucial as the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level of the HTM should be less negative than the perovskite for efficient hole extraction, whilst the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) should be sufficiently high to allow beneficial electron blocking. Furthermore, HTMs should have high hole mobility to facilitate efficient transport to the metal contact.
There are several aspects to the long-term stability in PSCs, including modification of the perovskite absorber, surface treatments/passivation, the development of improved device architectures, exploration of new additives, and so on.[36,45,46] Apart from these solutions, the development of novel dopant-free HTMs is also seen as one of the key aspects for long-term stable PSCs.
## 3.1. The Challenging Role of Dopants in Conventional Spiro-OMeTAD Hole Transport Materials
Although the PCE for PSC devices employing Spiro-OMeTAD as the HTM has been reported above $23\%$ ,[25] the reliance of HTMs on additives like bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI), 4 tert-butyl pyridine (tBP) or tris(2-(1H-pyrazol-1-yl)- 4-tert-butylpyridine)cobalt(III)tri[bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide] (FK209) has been unavoidable, particularly in terms of the well-known SBF-based Spiro-OMeTAD (as shown in Figure 6). Its twisted SBF-based molecule is the most conventionally used small organic molecule HTM responsible for high performance in mesoporous PSCs. The first employment of Spiro-OMeTAD as an HTM in devices was reported by Bach et al. in 1998.[27]
The cross-linked structure of Spiro-OMeTAD results in improved morphological stability at the same time retaining its electronic properties. However, the requirement for making Spiro-OMeTAD a highly effective HTM is to oxidize and dope it with additional salts. Pristine Spiro-OMeTAD suffers from low hole mobility $(\approx10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{S}^{-1})$ and lower hole conductivity $(\approx10^{-8}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ .[47,48] The low conductivity and hole mobility originate from the $\mathrm{SP}^{3}$ hybridized $\mathrm{~N~}$ atom with pyramidal structure with large intermolecular distances. This results in the movement of charges over long paths which lowers conductivity.[49] Additionally, Spiro-OMeTAD contains two types of steric hindrances, which negatively affect the stability of the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. The intermolecular steric hindrance between two molecules prevents formation of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking in addition to the hindrance of outer fragments of each cell which prevents formation of continuous $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking. Electronic doping is an effective way of tuning the type and density of charge carriers.[48,50,51] Finally, the ionization potential, also referred to as the electron affinity of the dopant with respect to the energy level of the host, plays a significant role in understanding the feasibility of the doping.
Dopants such as Li-TFSI,[51] Na-TFSI,[52] Ag-TFSI,[53] Co(III) complexes,[54] $\ensuremath{\mathbb{W}}\ensuremath{\operatorname{O}_{3}}$ ,[55] Cu (II)[56,57] salts when employed in Spiro-OMeTAD can generate charge carriers and tune electronic properties.[51,58] Lithium based salts are the first additives employed for Spiro-OMeTAD doping.[27] The role of Li salt is to control electrochemical properties of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which happens through absorption and intercalation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions.[27,59] Thus, Li-TFSI doping results in the dual improvement in the hole mobility of Spiro-OMeTAD through improved disorder and broadening of the density of states along with partial passivation of deep Coulomb traps which minimizes the charge transfer barrier.[48]
Another conventional dopant in Spiro-OMeTAD is tBP, which was employed for the first time in solid-state dye solar cells where it showed an enhanced $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and fill factor (FF). It is known that heat treatment of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ causes oxygen vacancies that are detrimental for solar cell performance.[60] tBP acts as a passivating agent for undercoordinated titanium atoms and reduces the charge recombination.[61] Additionally, both tBP and Li-TFSI improve miscibility of Spiro-OMeTAD.[62] However, in our recent studies,[63,64] we found that use of Li-TFSI and tBP dopants in Spiro-OMeTAD increases the hydrophilic nature of the Spiro-OMeTAD HTM and this induces rapid degradation of the devices. The hygroscopic property is governed by the salt-like nature of Li-TFSI dopants. Therefore, recently several approaches of undoped hole transport materials have been reported in the direction of making more stable PSCs.[63–65]
There have been several attempts to replace hygroscopic Li-TFSI dopants with more stable additives, which include the use of $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ pyridine,[56] F4-TCNQ,[66] CO (III),[67] $\mathtt{C u S C N}$ or CuI.[57] However, they still possess expensive complex synthesis processes and require preparation in high purity to obtain high efficiency PSCs.
In addition to the above disadvantages, the synthesis and purification of Spiro-OMeTAD is tedious, making it more expensive, about 400 USD per gram (note all $\$5$ values in this review are in US dollars unless specified).[63,68] Furthermore, other disadvantages include the sensitivity of the dopant’s concentration, instability in perovskite devices caused by their use, and their poor solubility in organic solvents and high volatility.[69] Thus, the abovementioned drawbacks encourage the development of alternative, more stable and cost-effective p-type HTMs as an active field of research for PSCs.
## 3.2. The Development of Organic Hole Transporting Materials
The HTL plays a significant role in PSCs, because a higher PCE can be achieved with an HTL. This is due to suppressed charge recombination and the fact that an HTL can act as an electron barrier.[7,65,70] In addition to the role of charge extraction, HTLs in inverted layouts are the growing substrate of choice for PSCs, which have a significant impact on the device performance via the HTLs influence on perovskite film formation.[71]
In general, HTMs for PSCs can be divided into inorganic and organic semiconductors. Compared to organic HTMs, inorganic HTMs can have simpler, thus lower materials cost, but they usually require deposition from more expensive nonsolution-based techniques such as sputtering,[72] atomic layer[73] or pulsed laser deposition.[74] In general, inorganic HTMs also have higher intrinsic hole mobility and thus they do not require any dopants or additives allowing better long-term stability. However, the major drawback is that the efficiency of most inorganic HTMsbased devices to date are lower than those of organic ones[68,75] and there is a limited number of choices available compared to the vast combinations for organic HTMs, such as those reviewed below.
Note this review differs from previous reviews on HTMs,[46,76,77] in that we specifically review only the undoped organic-based HTMs used for PSCs.
Overall, the number of novel HTMs reported with mesoporous devices is greater than those of planar and inverted ones. The development of the most efficient HTMs is summarized in Figure  11. Among them, the number of new polymers is less than those of other types of HTMs. Most small molecular HTMs were fabricated in $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ -based PSCs whereas many polymeric ones were employed in devices with other kinds of perovskite materials. Thus, there is room to develop polymeric HTMs for planar and inverted solar cells with triple mixed cation perovskite materials. Almost all reported small molecular HTMs have been designed with triphenylamine (TPA) as the end-capping groups. The following three sections are separated into (1) mesoporous, (2) planar, and (3) inverted, based on their PSC device configurations.
## 3.2.1. Mesoporous Devices
Very recently, Chang and co-workers functionalized a benzene core with two and three $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(1H-imidazole-4,5-diyl)bis(N,Nbis(4-methoxyphenyl)aniline) electron-rich substitutions.[78] While the reference doped Spiro-OMeTAD devices gave a PCE of $16.8\%$ , $N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime\prime},N^{\prime}$ -( $4,4^{\prime\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ - $(2,2^{\prime}$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis(1Himidazole-5,4,2-triyl))tetrakis(4,1-phenylene))tetrakis(4-methoxyN-(4-methoxyphenyl)benzenamine) (Ph-TPA-4A) as the HTM produced a PCE of $18.03\%$ under identical conditions. This is the highest efficiency recorded so far for pristine HTMs fabricated in mesoporous $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ devices. Importantly, the cost evaluation of Ph-TPA-4A was only $\mathord{\approx}\$11$ per gram, much lower than that of Spiro-OMeTAD. The report also showed that the linear symmetrical donor–donor–donor (D–D–D) will be the rational design for dopant-free HTMs. On the other hand, there are few asymmetric shapes of undoped HTMs reported. The resultant device data of the $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -(ethene-1,1,2-triyl)tris(N,N-bis(4-methoxyphenyl)aniline) (CJ-01) and 4-(2-ethylhexyl)-2-(5-(2-methylprop-1-en-1-yl)thiazol-2-yl)-4H-dithieno[3,2-b:2′,3′-d]pyrrole (ICTH2) was significant. While CJ-01-based devices displayed a PCE of $18.56\%$ in a small active layer, the PCE of ICTH2 HTM was $18.75\%$ with a working area of $2.18~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .[79,80]
Organic dye derivatives have been intensively applied for the development of cost-effective and printable solar cells. A few undoped HTM-based organic dyes for PSCs, such as anthanthrone (ANT),[63,65] quinacridone (QA),[64] and carbazole,[81] have been reported. While the PCE of $(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime})$ -tetrakis $(N,N^{\prime}$ -di-pmethoxyphenylamine)- $\mathbf{\Psi}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -bicarbazole (2,7 BCz-OMeTAD) HTM-based mesoporous $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.79}M A_{0.16}P b I_{2.49}B r_{0.51}}$ PSCs was $17.6\%$ with an active area of $1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $18.2\%$ efficiency for the mesoporous $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ devices using the HTM. Additionally, 2,9-bis(1,2-dihydroacenaphthylen-5-yl)-5,12-dioctyl-5,12-dihydroquinolino[2,3-b]acridine-7,14-dione (ACE–QA–ACE), was achieved on a lab-scale active area.
Besides small molecular HTMs for $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs, a few polymeric counterparts have been developed and successfully fabricated in the devices. Aside from conjugated D–A polymers reported for dopant-free HTMs and highly stable PSCs, poly[4- (5-(4,8-bis(5-(6-((2-hexyldecyl)oxy)naphthalen-2-yl)thiophen-2-yl) benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophen-2-yl)-4-octylthiophen-2-yl)-5,6-difluoro-7-(4-octylthiophen-2-yl)benzo[c][1,2,5]thiadiazole] (coded as P3) was engineered by combining the combination between benzo[1,2-b:4,5:b′]dithiophene (BDT) and 2,1,3-benzothiadiazole (BTD) moieties. Pristine P3-based PSCs displayed a high PCE of $17.28\%$ and exhibited excellent ambient stability up to 30 days with $95\%$ remaining from the initial efficiency. Meanwhile, P3 was also used as a donor material for additive-free bulk heterojunction (BHJ) organic solar cells (OSCs), showing a PCE of $8.26\%$ . The authors found that the enhanced face-on orientation, close $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, finely mixed active layer morphology, high hydrophobicity, and high charge carrier mobility strongly supported the high PCE and better stability of the additive free P3-based PSCs and BHJ OSCs. Later on, Park’s research group reported a green-solvent-processable HTM based on an asymmetric D–A polymer, functionalized 4,8-bis((2-butyloctyl) oxy)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophene and BTD (namely, asy$\mathrm{PBTBDT}_{2\cdot\mathrm{MA}}^{}$ ).[82] A robust PSC with $18.3\%$ efficiency with $91\%$ retention after 30 days without encapsulation under $50\mathrm{-}75\%$ relative humidity without dopants was shown. It is a first report for a green-solvent-processable hole-transporting polymer, unveiling the highest efficiencies reported so far for n–i–p $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ devices without dopants.
In the case of solar cells using mixed perovskite materials, an efficient D–A–D type hole transport material was designed by using the planarity core of the cyclopenta[hi]aceanthrylene group and triphenylamine, named $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(cyclopenta[hi] aceanthrylene-2,7-diyl)bis(N,N-bis(4-methoxyphenyl)aniline) (YN3).[83] The $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ PSCs devices based on YN3 as a pristine HTM exhibited impressive PCEs of $18.84\%$ and $12.05\%$ , respectively. Particularly in the same ageing test condition, while the devices based on doped Spiro-OMeTAD kept $57\%$ of the initial efficiency, YN3- based $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ PSCs was able to maintain $92\%$ of its initial efficiency. A higher performance of $19\%$ was reported by Rakstys et  al. A donor– $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\pi$ -bridge–acceptor $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{D-}\pi\mathrm{-}\mathrm{A})$ type of HTM, called $(2,2^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime},$ -(((5,10,15-trihexyl-10,15-dihydro$5H_{\l}$ -diindolo $[3,2\cdot\mathsf{a};3^{\prime},2^{\prime}$ -c]carbazole-3,8,13-triyl)tris $^{3,3^{\prime\prime}}$ -dihexyl$[2,2^{\prime};5^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -terthiophene]- $.5^{\prime\prime}$ ,5-diyl))tris(methanylylidene))
trimalononitrile) (KR321), was synthesized successfully.[84] This HTM based on triazatruxene, thiophene, and malononitrile moieties was fabricated in $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ devices, which rendered a PCE of $19\%$ without any dopants. Apart from small molecules, Zhang et  al. designed a simple structured copolymer, 2,5-dialkoxyl-1,4-phenylene unit and bithiophene unit, (DTB) with 2,5-dialkoxyl-1,4-phenylene unit and bithiophene unit.[85] The PCE using undoped DTB led to a superior passivation effect to the defects on the surface of the perovskite, resulting in a higher extraction efficiency of $19.5\%$ compared with the typical Spiro-OMeTAD $(19.81\%)$ under identical conditions. Moreover, the undoped DTB devices exhibited long-term stability. More importantly, the unusual edge-on molecular arrangement was realized for the first time for highly efficient polymers, and DTB was of rather simple structure without fused rings, contrary to the previous highly efficient polymers that were of very complicated molecular structures.
From the first report in 2016, cesium-containing triple cation PSCs have attracted much attention. Saliba et al. revealed that “triple cation” (Cs/MA/FA) mesoporous configurations with doped Spiro-OMeTAD resulted in a stabilized power output of $21.1\%$ and around $18\%$ after $250\mathrm{~h~}$ under operational conditions.[86] After that, there were a few novel organic HTMs employed in this type of PSCs. Aung and co-workers developed a new HTM, $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylaniline)- $\alpha,$ $\beta$ -cyclooct $\iota[1,2.\mathrm{b};4,3.\mathrm{b}^{\prime};5,6.\mathrm{b}^{\prime};8,7.\mathrm{b}^{\prime\prime\prime}.$ tetrathiophenyl (COTh-Ph-OMeTAD), by utilizing a cyclooctatetrathiophene (COTh) as a saddle-shaped motif as the core and four triphenylamine units as arms.[87] After fabrication in the devices, COTh- $\cdot\mathrm{Ph}$ -OMeTAD exhibited an efficiency of $17.22\%$ which is a little higher than that of devices based on doped SpiroOMeTAD $(\approx16.83\%)$ under similar working conditions.
Remarkably, the PSCs with dopant-free COTh-Ph-OMeTAD as both the HTM and interfacial layer presented better stability so the PCE could still be maintained at $86\%$ of the maximum after being stored in the dark and dry air without encapsulation for nearly $800\mathrm{h}$ . Following up to the DTB polymer for mixed PSCs, Zhang et  al. successfully synthesized a polymeric HTM engineered by partially introducing $3\%$ diethylene glycol (DEG) groups in DTB polymer. This was proposed to simultaneously optimize the properties of both the bulk of the HTM layer and the HTM/perovskite interface.[88] A champion PCE of $20.19\%$ with a certified value of $20.10\%$ was achieved after DTB (3DEG) was used as a pristine HTM for triple cation PSCs. This PCE was the first one over $20\%$ reported for dopant-free small molecular and polymeric HTMs employed in mesoporous layouts. Importantly, the champion device retained superior long-term stability to the rest where the PCE were operational with $92.25\%$ performance retention after being kept in air for 30 days without any encapsulation.
## 3.2.2. Planar Devices
Three novel small molecules by fusing two strong electron donating moieties, $4H$ -dithieno[3,2-b:2′,3′-d]pyrrole (DBTP) and triphenylamine, were reported as dopant-free HTMs in FTO/ZnO/WFP/MAPbI3/HTL/Au. Among them, the devices with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(11-hexyl-11H-benzo[4,5]thieno[3,2-b]benzo[4,5] thieno[2,3- $d]$ pyrrole-3,8-diyl)bis(N,N-bis(3-methoxyphenyl)aniline) (mDPA-DBTP) as the HTM showed a PCE of $18.09\%$ with negligible hysteresis, which were higher than a device using doped Spiro-OMeTAD $(17.82\%)$ .[89] Importantly, while the doped Spiro-OMeTAD devices lost their PCE completely after 33 days of storage, the dopant-free mDPA-DBTP device retained $81\%$ of its original PCE. The higher hydrophobicity and lack of additional dopants in the mDPA-DBTP effectively improved the air storage stability of the devices.
Kim et al. synthesized and deployed successfully a promising conjugated D–A polymer RCP, comprising of BDT and BTD, as a dopant-free HTM in the configuration $\mathrm{FTO/SnO_{2}/M A P b I_{3}/}$ HTM/Au. The RCP-based PSCs provided a high efficiency of $17.3\%$ and maintained their initial efficiency for over $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ at $75\%$ humidity, whereas devices made with additives failed after $900\ \mathrm{{h.}^{[90]}}$ Additional polymers were designed by using carbazole and benzothiadiazole (BT)-based donor–acceptor copolymers, which have different lengths of alkoxy side-chains grafted on the BT unit, and they were fabricated as the dopantfree HTMs for the glass/ $\mathrm{\primeITO/TiO_{2}/P C_{60}B M/M A P b I_{3}/H T M s/}$ $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Au}$ device structure. Apart from them, PCDTBT1-based devices gave an impressive PCE of $19.1\%$ , which was attributed to the more effective suppression of charge recombination at the perovskite/PCDTBT1 interface by coupling the passivation effect from the thiophene and methoxy units.[91] This report also shows that the storage lifetime of this device is also superior, due to its hydrophobic surface that prevents water penetration, as well as the lower density of trap sites.
Li’s research group has utilized the fluoranthene unit, which has highly planar structure, easy functionality, low price, and good thermal/electrochemical stability, for the development new class of fluoranthene-cored HTMs.[92,93] A first series of fluoranthene-based HTMs were functionalized by methoxysubstituted TPA units on either the 3-, 8-positions or the 3-, 8-, 9-positions to form linear and star-shaped structures. Subsequently, these undoped HTMs were employed in a device configuration of $\mathrm{ITO/C_{60}/M A P b I_{\it x}C l_{3-x}/H T L/M o O_{3}/A g}$ . Among them, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -( $(1E,1^{\prime}E)$ -fluoranthene-3,8-diylbis(ethene-2,1-diyl)) bis(N,N-bis(4-methoxyphenyl)aniline) (FBA3), with a low lab synthesis cost of only $\$14.75$ per gram, delivered an impressive PCE of $19.27\%$ .[92] Under identical ageing tests, while over $90\%$ of the initial PCEs can be retained for all the dopant-free devices, that of the doped Spiro-OMeTAD decayed to $75\%$ of its initial value. On the other hand, a typical HTM, 3,8-bis(bis(4- methoxyphenyl)amino)fluoranthene-4,5-dicarbonitrile (BTF4), based on a rigid planarized structure of 2,3-dicyano-fluoranthene moieties was synthesized and employed in planar and inverted $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ perovskite devices. BTF4- based devices displayed promising efficiencies, which were found to be $18.03\%$ for planar structures and $17.01\%$ for inverted ones.[93] Moreover, BTF4-based inverted devices exhibited an enhanced stability in both devices. The research also shows the presence of two cyano units which may result in higher device performance.
Tang’s research group has found that the utilization of dithieno $\cdot[3,2\cdot b:2^{\prime},3^{\prime}\cdot d]$ pyrrole (DTP) units as the donor central core in the D–D–D geometry of HTMs achieved an excellent efficiency in $\mathrm{FTO/SnO_{2}/C_{60^{-}}S A M/M A_{0.7}F A_{0.3}P b I_{2.85}B r_{0.15}/}$ PMMA/HTL/Au configurations. Indeed, the resultant data of $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -((4-octyl- $^{4H}$ -dithieno $3,2\cdot b:2^{\prime},3^{\prime}\cdot d]$ pyrrole-2,6-diyl) bis(thiophene-5,2,3-triyl))tetrakis(N,N-bis(4-methoxyphenyl) aniline) (DTPC8-ThDTPA), containing a dithieno $[3,2-b;2^{\prime},3^{\prime}-d]$ pyrrole core flanked with two-fold triphenylamine arm substituted thiophene bridges, as a dopant-free HTM showed a PCE of $19.42\%$ .[94] The DTPC8-ThDTPA molecule exhibited a higher glass transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ , better hydrophobicity, deeper ionization potential, and higher hole mobility when compared to doped Spiro-OMeTAD. Later on, the DTPC8-ThDTPA structure was modified by replacing four TPA arm units with two TPA ones, but the device performance was reduced to $18.37\%$ .[95] Nevertheless, the replacement of n-octyl chains by 5-ethyldodecane branched ones gave amazing results. In particular, the PCE of devices using $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -((4-(7-ethylundecyl)-4Hdithieno[ $3,2\cdot\mathrm{b}{:}2^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ -d]pyrrole-2,6-diyl)bis(thiophene-5,2-diyl)) bis(N,N-bis(4-methoxyphenyl)aniline) (DTPC13-ThTPA) as undoped HTMs was found to be $20.38\%$ .[95] Moreover, the synthesis cost of DTPC13-ThTPA was evaluated to be $\$14.36$ per gram. In this report, the longer branched alkyl chain lead to, not only a larger HOMO energy level in films, but also a smoother film surface and higher hole mobility, which is in good accordance with previous attempts.[96,97]
For undoped polymeric HTMs deployed in the $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ perovskite architecture, a series of polymers, featuring benzodithiophene and/or bis(alkyloxy)benzene moieties, were synthesized successfully.[98] Among them, P1-based devices achieved a PCE of $18.30\%$ , which is attributed to planar polymer backbones and alkylthienyl conjugated side chains of P1. In addition, while the efficiency of SpiroOMeTAD-based devices degraded rapidly and only $51.3\%$ of the initial efficiency remains after $96\mathrm{~h~}$ , the devices based on P1
HTM still maintained an efficiency over $89\%$ due to stronger hydrophobicity.
Along with $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ perovskite layouts, the application of dopant-free polymers into triple-cation CsFAMA perovskite devices has achieved outstanding performance. For example, two side-chain polystyrenes with triphenylamine substituents on carbazole moieties, denoted as P1 and P2, were designed and characterized. Apart from two polymers, the polymer P2 with the TPA substituent on the 3,6-positions of the carbazole unit showed higher performance with PCE of $18.45\%$ , which was much higher than the PCE $(16.78\%)$ of P1 with 2,7-positions substituted.[99] Additionally, using the nonchlorine green solvent of 2-methylanisole, P2-based devices displayed a PCE of $16.42\%$ , which is slightly lower than that of the device using chlorobenzene as the solvent. The report pointed out that side-chain polymers provide promising HTMs for PSC applications and the performance of side-chain polymers could be optimized by carefully tuning the structure of the monomer. In another example, Park and colleagues explored the PTEG polymer, which mainly consists of BDT and 2,1,3-benzothiadiazole (BT), which revealed an excellent performance with an overall PCE of $19.8\%$ , the highest efficiency compared with all reported $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ planar devices.[100]
## 3.2.3. Inverted Planar Devices
Compared to n–i–p layouts, inverted (p–i–n) architectures have some advantages: (1) the fabrication process can be carried out at low temperature; (2) negligible or hysteresis-free behaviors are characterized; (3) the thickness of HTLs is thinner than that of $\mathrm{{\n-i-p}}$ architectures, which can therefore offer lower production cost.[24,101,102]
The most commonly used HTM for inverted planar PSCs is poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] or better known as PTAA as shown in Figure  12. Stolterfoht et  al.[103] were able to demonstrate high efficiency inverted PSCs with $20.4\%$ and $18.9\%$ for actives areas of 0.06 and $1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, using dopant-free thin PTAA layers as their HTM. Even though PTAA shows better stability at higher temperatures compared to most other HTMs, in some cases it still requires dopants to reach high efficiency. More importantly, it is also prohibitively expensive and would be unsuitable for large scale manufacturing.
Very recently, Tsang and co-workers reported two novel D– $\cdot\pi$ –D HTMs, containing 4,8-di(hexylthio)- BDT as $\pi$ -conjugated linker, and $N$ -(6-bromohexyl) phenothiazine (PTZ)/N-(6-bromohexyl) phenoxazine (POZ) as donor units. They were employed in inverted $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ PSCs as dopant-free HTMs. While the device using 10-(6-bromohexyl)-3-(6-(10-(6-bromohexyl)- $10H$ -phenothiazin-3-yl)-4,8-Di(hexylthio)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophene-2-yl)- $10H$ -phenothiazine (BDT-PTZ) as the HTM displayed a PCE of $18.26\%$ , that of 10-(6-bromohexyl)-3-(6-(10-(6-bromohexyl)- $10H$ -phenoxazin-3-yl)-4,8-Di(hexylthio)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophene-2-yl)- $\cdot10H$ -phenoxazine (BDT-POZ) was even higher, reaching $19.16\%$ .[104] Notably, BDT-POZ-based devices demonstrated a superior fill factor of $81.7\%$ and retained about $80\%$ of its initial PCE even after $400\mathrm{~h~}$ . By contrast, the PCE of PTAA devices were operational with merely $66\%$ performance retention under the same testing conditions. Another impressive efficiency was also reached by Zhang and co-authors. They found a dopant-free HTM based on 9,9-dihexyl- $^{9H}$ -fluorene and $N,N$ -dip-methylthiophenylamine (denoted as FMT) deployed in p–i–n $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ layouts, which showed a PCE of $19.06\%$ , whereas a reference PEDOT:PSS HTM-based device exhibited an efficiency of $13.9\%$ under the same fabrication and working conditions.[105] Moreover, the storage stability of FMT HTL-based devices at 60 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ environment retained about $59\%$ of its initial PCE after $132\mathrm{h}$ . The synthesis cost of FMT is also roughly $\$41.8$ per gram, which is much less expensive than that of PEDOT:PSS. For inverted structures, PEDOT:PSS does have the advantage that it does not need to be salt-doped like in spiro-OMeTAD or in some cases PTAA, which accelerates degradation. This was shown to be beneficial especially for inverted $\mathrm{\DeltaPb{-}}S_{\mathrm{n}}$ mixed triple cation PSCs with a champion efficiency $16.10\%$ .[106] The champion device managed to retain $93\%$ of its PCE after 30 days in air, which is quite remarkable as Sn-based perovskites are known to oxidize from $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ in air over time.
Compared to several small molecular counterparts, there are two other notable polymeric HTMs fabricated in inverted devices. Hong’s group members developed novel conjugate polymers of BDT and 4,4-difluoro-4-bora- $3a,4a$ -diaza-s-indacene (BODIPY) (coded as K1) for use as HTMs without dopants.[107] The PCE of the devices using K1 as the HTM was around $16\%$ . Furthermore, the exceptional solar cell stability was shown by the fact that over $80\%$ of its original PCE value was maintained after 10 days under ambient air conditions. Meanwhile, other researchers reported three polymeric HTMs with alternating phenyl and dithiophene backbones but different side chain end groups, namely, PB2T-O, PB2T-S, and PB2T-SO. Application of these polymers into the $\mathrm{ITO/HTL/MAPbI_{3}/P C_{61}B M/Z n O/A l}$ showed that PB2T-O with weak binding group $\left(\mathrm{-OCH}_{3}\right)$ to Pb, gave both improved photovoltaic performance of $16.7\%$ with negligible hysteresis in devices.[108] In comparison with other PCEs of CsFAMA solar cells, a new HTM deployed in p–i–n devices has made a breakthrough recently by Wang and colleagues. Two novel V-shape and linear HTMs, that were denoted as MPA5-BTI and MPABTTI, respectively, were successfully designed and deployed as undoped HTMs in triple cation PSCs.[109] Amazingly, the PCE of
MPA-BTTI-based devices reached to $21.17\%$ . The resultant data exhibited negligible hysteresis and superior thermal and longterm stability under illumination. The outstanding PCE, which is attributed to the well-aligned energy levels, appropriate hole mobility, and most importantly, the excellent film morphology of the MPA-BTTI, breaks the long-time standing bottleneck in the development of dopant-free small molecular HTMs for highly efficient inverted architectures. Additionally, the device performance suggests that the linear molecular structure is better than a V-shape or twisted one, which is in good agreement with previous attempts.[110] Besides this, Wong and co-workers fabricated successfully large-area (aperture $1.00\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) devices using dopantfree XY1, which was synthesized by utilizing four methoxy substituted triphenylamine groups on the small thieno[2,3-b]thiophene core through a single-step reaction.[101] The best PCE of $17.82\%$ was one of the most efficient large-area inverted PSCs based on organic HTMs. Meanwhile, hysteresis free small scaled devices based on XY1 showed a competitive PCE of $18.78\%$ . Further, several novel chemical structures of dopant-free HTMs with moderate device data have been reported (summarized in Table 1 and 2) yet not discussed in detail here.[111–153]
## 4. Discussion
## 4.1. The Effect of Deposition Methods on Device Performance
Nearly all the HTMs discussed thus far have been deposited via solution-based techniques. Another area that has been quite promising, although less explored, is vacuum deposited HTMs. This is most commonly achieved through thermal evaporation. The advantages include, excellent surface uniformity, low film roughness, and of course the ability to deposit the HTMs solution free (thus potentially avoiding all the toxic solvents typically employed for solution processing of PSCs). Polander et al. in 2014, were able to deposit a range of HTMs including $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -Tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]- ${\mathfrak{g}},{\mathfrak{g}}^{\prime}$ - spirobifluorene (Spiro-MeO-TAD), N4,N4,N4′,N4′-Tetrakis(4- methoxyphenyl)-[1,1′-biphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diamine (MeO-TPD), 2,7-bis[N,N-bis(4-methoxy-phenyl)amino]-9,9-spirobifluorene (Spiro-MeO-TPD), $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis-[N,N-di(4-methylphenyl) amino]-9,9′-spirobifluorene (Spiro-TTB), and $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,N-diphenylamino)-9,9′-spirobifluorene (Spiro-TAD) via thermal evaporation to demonstrate fully vacuum deposited inverted p–i–n PSCs with efficiency up to $10.9\%$ .[154] However, doping of the HTMs with F6-TCNNQ or NDP9 was still required. Elsewhere, Bolink’s team, pioneers in vacuum deposition of PSCs, were able to demonstrate dopant-free thermally evaporated ( $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -tris[phenyl(m-tolyl)amino]triphenylamine (m-MTDATA), tris(4-carbazoyl-9-ylphenyl)amine (TcTa) and $N4,N4,N4^{\prime\prime},N4^{\prime\prime}$ -tetra([1,1′-biphenyl]-4-yl)- $[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $4{,}4{^{\prime\prime}}$ -diamine (TaTm) HTMs on $\mathrm{MoO}_{x},$ achieving $14.4\%$ , $12.2\%$ , and $16.3\%$ , respectively[155] as well as with the HTM, 5,10,15-tribenzyl- ${.5H}$ -diindolo $[3,2\cdot\mathrm{a};3^{\prime},2^{\prime}$ -c]-carbazole (TBDI) on $\mathrm{MoO}_{x}$ achieving $14.85\%$ .[156] HTMs deposited via thermal evaporation have an additional advantage, because they can be coated conformally onto nonflat surfaces, for example, textured crystalline silicon, with dopant-free Spiro-TTB in perovskitesilicon tandem solar cells.[157] Table 3 provides some examples of thermally evaporated HTMs for PSCs.
## 4.2. Molecular Engineering of Novel Organic Hole Transporting Materials
To date, the number of publications relevant to newly developed small molecular HTMs is higher than that of polymeric ones as shown in Figure 13a. Meanwhile, the PCEs of devices using small molecular HTMs is barely higher than those of layouts utilizing polymeric ones.
If we examine just the symmetrical small molecular HTMs reported to date, these can be further categorized into linear, star-shaped (3D), and spiro-orthogonal (4D) geometries.[68] Based on highly efficient PSCs using these HTMs in this review, the number of linear structures make up the majority of the novel HTMs while those of star-shaped (3D) and spiro-orthogonal (4D) ones form around $17-18\%$ of new HTMs (as illustrated in Figure 14a,b). Particularly, as shown in Figure  14b, the highest PCE was reported by using the linear HTMs.
According to our literature review, most of the reported compounds exhibit very high decomposition temperature. Additionally, some claim that the higher $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ of novel HTMs compared to that of Spiro-OMeTAD is beneficial for the PSCs, particularly in terms of device stability because the high $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ leads to the improvement of the amorphous state of HTMs.[158] However, other reports show that the $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ does not affect the efficiency and stability of devices. For example, even though TPA–ANT– TPA,[63] ACE–QA–ACE,[64] KR321,[84] YN3,[83] ICTH2,[80] FBA3,[92] HTM3,[135] mTPA-DBTP,[89] TPA–TVT—TPA,[138] BDT–POZ,[104] and so forth possess either low $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ or undetectable $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ compared to that of Spiro-OMeTAD, the device stability and performance using these HTMs are still better than those of the doped reference Spiro-OMeTAD under identical condition. From analysis of data from thermal studies of dopant-free HTMs, mainly of which show amorphous morphology, indicates this leads to preferred stability which may be an advantageous property for potential HTMs in highly efficient PSCs.
There are observations to be made with respect to the requirements for high hole mobility in dopant free HTMs. Many of the dopant free HTMs exhibit hole mobilities in the magnitude range of $10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which is comparable to that of Spiro-OMeTAD $(\approx2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ as opposed to inorganic HTMs such as CuI or $\mathrm{CuSCN}(10\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . Several of the structures, irrespective of the device architecture, show reasonable performance and equivalent hole mobilities of the order $10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which is on the higher range of typical polymeric HTMs $(10^{-3}{-}10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . Interestingly, it should be noted exceptional examples included PTEG $(16.4\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}).$ ,[87] P1 $(17.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}),$ [85] M118 $(17.5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ ,[84] pBBTa-BDT2 $(20\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ ,[4] RCP $(30.9\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}),$ ,[77] and R1 $(37\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ .[131] Structural observations showed that few were purely of a nitrogen heterocyclic nature, i.e., M118, with several of the more exceptional performances containing thiophene, i.e., pBBTa-BDT2, and several with extended conjugation and polymeric backbones, i.e., R1, PTEG, and RCP, which showed enhanced performance over small molecular and larger branched molecular structures. This indicates that high hole mobility $(10^{-1}{-}10^{-2}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ in dopant free HTMs may not be as critical as achieving optimum structures that extend conjugation with functional groups that enable better interaction between device layers.
Overall, the newly synthesized organic materials, which can be ideal hole transporting materials for PSCs, should satisfy features including high purity, amorphous nature, high thermal ability, appropriate HOMO–LUMO energy levels, and good solution processability (as shown in Figure 15). Moreover, the new materials should possess high water contact angles, which can improve the stability of the devices.
## 5. Conclusions and Outlook
In this review, a critical assessment of the challenges in stability and large-scale production associated with the use of conventional Spiro-OMeTAD hole transport materials is addressed with a focus on alternative, nondoped, HTMs for application in the PSC. The review summarizes recent literature reports on nondoped, alternative and more stable HTMs used in PSCs as essential components for high-efficiency cells, regardless of cell architecture. Dopant-free polymeric HTMs, such as PTAA, have recently shown good stability along with high efficiency, yet issues still remain with respect to reproducible chemical synthesis, reliable deposition, and most importantly relatively higher cost of preparation. Equally, many inorganic HTMs have been investigated for PSCs, yet reported performances still lag that of organic-based HTMs. In this review, we categorized HTMs as reported for PSC architectures (mesoporous, planar, and inverted) in addition to dopant-free small molecule and polymeric HTM materials. Further, we reviewed the use of thermal evaporation as an alternative method for depositing HTMs as continued investigation of tandem PSC architectures will require a method for depositing such materials sequentially, uniformly, and increasingly in the absence of solvents. It is evident from our review that an essential area of investigation is continued research and development of low-cost and effective HTMs as an alternative to widely reported incumbent conventional materials, such as doped spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA, which are both high cost and less stable, properties required for widespread utilization and commercialization. Although effective for achieving high cell PCE, Spiro-OMeTAD is relatively expensive due to a complex synthetic process and requires doping with hygroscopic additives to achieve reported performances, yet equally leading to concerns for long term stability of PSCs. There is distinct sensitivity and concentration dependency of dopants in Spiro-OMeTAD, leading to reproducibility issues in efficiency of PSCs prepared using this
HTM. Conversely, there are many reports of the use of donor/ acceptor structures for the development of dopant-free HTMs. Ideally, dopant-free HTMs need to be effective and low-cost, prepared by facile synthesis processes, in high-purity and high-yield, possess high hole mobility and appropriate energy (HUMO/LUMO) levels and demonstrate long-term stability. Furthermore, dopant-free HTMs should be able to be deposited reproducibly and uniformly over large areas using an industrially scalable methodology if a commercial application is envisioned for PSCs. This review presents a timely account of recent reports for dopant-free HTMs and provides insight into the development of stable PSC devices.
Keywords
dopant-free, hole transporting materials, perovskite solar cells, stability
